comments[12]:
  - created_at: "2026-11-17T00:41:32.325Z"
    author: "harness:claude-code"
    text: "Residuals for the adversarial corpus, from the pm-ycgb5g and pm-3fbj1f review loop (Codex rounds 1 to 3, 2026-11-17). (1) password_assignment follows references nested one level in an unquoted value. A literal default nested three or more levels deep (password=${A:-${B:-${C:-<literal>}}}), or a literal after such a reference, is missed in the flat and quoted-key spellings, while quoted values and elements follow any depth through expandRuntimeReferences. Closing it needs a code tokenizer in place of the declared regex value grammar. (2) An unquoted password value may hold at most four whitespace-free quoted pieces; a literal spread over more pieces is missed. That bound keeps each structured scan to a few later keys. (3) A bare $VAR is still read as literal text by password_assignment and as a reference by credential_key_assignment; pm-hw00c2 owns aligning the two.\n"
  - created_at: "2026-11-17T01:18:05.330Z"
    author: "harness:claude-code"
    text: "Progress from the pm-ycgb5g branch (not closing this item): the second criterion, judging escaped JSON literals on their decoded original, is implemented for every key-named rule. decodeQuotedLiteral returns both the neutralized reading the value grammar reads and the decoded original at the same length. Capture offsets (d flag) map the matched span back, and acceptsSecretMatch then judges the original, so credential_key_assignment measures entropy on the decoded original value. The other criteria remain open: a declared adversarial corpus with a measured escape rate, and env-style all-caps keys.\n"
  - created_at: "2026-11-17T02:43:46.888Z"
    author: "harness:claude-code"
    text: "Residual (4), from the CodeRabbit review of PR 1745: an escaped quoted literal followed directly by an unquoted suffix in the same value (a quoted key whose value is an escaped literal glued to eight more literal characters) has only the escaped literal judged. The suffix is never measured, and the flat rule cannot match a quoted key. Main behaves the same. Closing it needs a mixed literal model: the decoded literal's characters are data and the suffix is text with shell quoting.\n"
  - created_at: "2026-11-17T04:53:01.572Z"
    author: "harness:claude-code"
    text: "Residual (5), from the CodeRabbit review of PR 1745: a password value's quoted piece ends at its first closing quote, even inside a reference, and the cut reference contributes nothing. So in a double-quoted value that holds a command reference with its own double quotes, a literal after that inner quote is not measured. Closing it needs a quoted-value scan that follows references, done in code rather than by the declared regex grammar.\n"
  - created_at: "2026-11-17T05:32:50.567Z"
    author: "harness:claude-code"
    text: "Residual (6), from the Greptile review of PR 1745: every repeated group of the password value grammar is bounded at 4096 iterations, so a value is read up to 4096 pieces (runs, references, quoted pieces). A literal placed after 4096 leading references is not judged. The bound keeps every scan stack-safe; unbounded, a value of millions of references overflows the backtrack stack and the guard fails open. Main never judged a literal after a reference at all, so this is a limit of the new check, not a regression. Closing it needs a scan in code that continues past the bound without reopening the cross-key cost the quoted-piece bound prevents.\n"
  - created_at: "2026-11-17T08:40:29.119Z"
    author: "harness:claude-code"
    text: "Residual (7), from the credential-key reference work (2026-11-17): credential_key_assignment reads references and quoted pieces only when they are whitespace-free, as every credential-key value is. So a spaced command substitution followed directly by a literal is not judged under a credential key, while password_assignment, which allows spaced references, judges it. Spaced references stay excluded on purpose: a key inside a spaced command substitution then keeps its own verdict. A second gap found in the same work was a password assignment inside a spaced command substitution of another password value, which went unvisited once the whole-value password grammar landed; it is not a residual: pm-bn3ppf owns it and fixes it in the same change by judging each key inside a rejected reference value once."
    edited_at: "2026-11-17T08:47:31.294Z"
  - created_at: "2026-11-17T11:59:06.974Z"
    author: "harness:claude-code"
    text: "Residuals (8) and (9), from the CodeRabbit review of PR 1746 (2026-11-17); both are recorded limits, and main misses both. (8) credential_key_assignment rescans a rejected referenced value one level deep (shallow), so a referenced credential nested two command substitutions deep is judged only by the reference-free reading, which treats a dollar-led value as a placeholder. A deep rescan would be quadratic for entropy-rejected chains, because a credential key with its separator can be shorter than the literal minimum; closing it needs a depth-capped rescan with its own linear-time proof. (9) A credential-key value led by a lowercase dollar-name followed by a quoted literal keeps the reference-free reading, where a dollar-led literal is a placeholder. Only CONSTANT_CASE names are read as leading references. Closing it would mean judging the literal with the leading name removed for the grammar but kept for length and entropy; each such widening needs its own false-positive measurement over the tracker corpus first (pm-1l85at)."
  - created_at: "2026-11-17T14:02:42.100Z"
    author: "harness:claude-code"
    text: "Residuals (10) to (12), from the Greptile review of PR 1746 (2026-11-17); main behaves the same in all three. (10) A credential-key literal after a reference that ends with an opening parenthesis is read as a call and not judged; the shape is indistinguishable from a function call in source text. (11) A command substitution whose inner option name is quoted is visited by the structured pass as a quoted key, outside the rescan's enclosing-reference context, so a bare variable there keeps main's literal reading and is reported. (12) Inside a command substitution, a closing brace after a quoted inner variable is taken for the reference's own delimiter, so a literal suffix glued after it is not judged. Closing (11) and (12) needs the enclosing reference boundary carried into every match a rescan or structured walk makes inside a rejected value."
  - created_at: "2026-11-17T16:34:35.266Z"
    author: "harness:claude-code"
    text: "Residuals (13) and (14), from the Greptile review of PR 1746; main judges neither shape, because it treats every dollar-led credential-key value as a placeholder. (13) After a reference, an exclamation mark followed by a dot, an operator or a call reads as a TypeScript non-null assertion, so a YAML literal that starts with those characters right after a reference is not judged. (14) In the referenced reading each single ampersand is its own piece, so with more than 2,048 single ampersands the value is read only up to the 4,096-piece bound, the same stack-safety bound as residual (6) for the password grammar."
  - created_at: "2026-11-17T16:58:49.330Z"
    author: "harness:claude-code"
    text: "Residual (15), from the CodeRabbit review of PR 1746: an escaped dollar sign in a decoded string literal decodes to a dollar sign, so a whole-value escaped variable reads as a bare variable and is a placeholder. A shell reader would keep that dollar literal, but a JavaScript string or template literal yields the plain dollar sign; preserving the escape flagged template-literal fixtures with escaped interpolation. Closing it needs the host language of the surrounding text."
  - created_at: "2026-11-17T17:45:59.560Z"
    author: "harness:claude-code"
    text: "Residual (16), from Codex review round 34 on PR 1746: some credential-key exclusion patterns (hyphenated words, paths, unquoted member chains) still exhaust the regex stack on values of millions of characters, as they already did in 2026.11.17. Since PR 1746 such a value fails closed for itself (reported under the rule with normalization_incomplete) instead of failing the whole scan open. Making those exclusions stack-safe, for example by scanning them in code, would remove the review noise for such values."
  - created_at: "2026-11-23T21:45:30.751Z"
    author: "harness:claude-code"
    text: "Residuals (17) to (28), from the pm-gh1775 and pm-gh1773 work (2026-11-23/24, after Codex rounds 1-5 (loop cut at round 5 by the convergence rule), Greptile rounds 1-2 and CodeRabbit round 1); main behaves the same or worse in each. (17) In serialized JSON text, a credential-key literal attached to a dollar-name reference behind an ESCAPED quote (the JSON spelling of a shell concatenation inside a string value) is not judged: the value grammar stops at the backslash, and the structured pass judges only quoted-key values. The 2026.11.23 build misses it too. (18) The lexical string pass ends every string at a line feed or carriage return, so a multi-line double-quoted YAML scalar whose later line holds a bare reference assignment is still judged as before (reported). (19) URL userinfo that holds whitespace is not joined when it is a bare token with no colon anywhere, or when its first word looks like a dotted or localhost host and port (a dotted user, a colon and digits, then a blank); a colon that a space follows counts only when the separator's host goes on into a path, so a spaced password before a host without a path stays split. (20) A base64 password that holds a slash and a blank is split at the slash, which ends the spaced-userinfo search (a single token is still masked through its last at sign). (21) A credential URL followed within 16 words by prose holding an at sign (an email) is redacted through that at sign, as a URL parser reads the authority; prose context there is lost, never a credential. (22) A special scheme written without its slashes, or with a tab between the colon and the slashes, is not joined across a blank in its userinfo (the single-token rule still masks it when no blank splits it). (23) A spaced userinfo whose first word holds an encoded or plain at sign of a username but no colon is not joined; only the fragment before that at sign is masked. (24) A JSON-escaped quote inside a spaced password in serialized JSON text reads as a path delimiter and ends the search. (25) A spaced password colon before a host without a path is joined only when the quote that opened the URL closes it; an unquoted pathless URL with a spaced colon stays split. (26) Resolved after Greptile review round 3: an empty password (a colon directly before the at sign) and a numeric username part are now joined; only clock-shaped words are kept apart. (27) A bracketed username (an authority that starts with a square bracket) is treated as an IPv6 host and not joined; a literal backslash inside a non-special scheme's spaced userinfo is read as a path delimiter. (28) A credential-key value of millions of characters can still exhaust the V8 regex stack in the reference-free grammar (residual 16 class, main as well); scanMutationSecrets fails closed on it."
    edited_at: "2026-11-23T23:56:40.079Z"
body: ""
